  {"type": "split", "h2": "What AFUE, two-stage and variable-speed actually mean", "image": "Midea two-stage, variable-speed high-efficiency gas furnace installed by EHI", "image_src": "/assets/img/work/midea-gas-furnace-installed.webp", "image_fit": "cover", "image_credit": "EHI install", "image_pos": "50% 55%", "html": "<p><strong>AFUE</strong> (Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency) is the share of gas that becomes heat in your home over a season. A 96% AFUE furnace turns 96 cents of every gas dollar into heat. Older furnaces run 60% to 80%.</p><p><strong>Condensing</strong> furnaces hit those numbers with a second heat exchanger that pulls extra heat from the exhaust until water vapour condenses out. The exhaust is cool enough to vent through PVC, and the condensate runs to a drain.</p><p><strong>Single-stage</strong> furnaces are on or off at full fire. <strong>Two-stage</strong> furnaces, like the Lennox ML296V, EL297V and Midea MGH96, run at about 65% on mild days and only step up to full fire in a deep cold snap. <strong>Modulating</strong> furnaces adjust in smaller steps for the steadiest temperatures, at a higher price.</p><p>A <strong>variable-speed ECM blower</strong> matches airflow to the stage, uses a fraction of the electricity of an old PSC motor, and runs quietly on low for better filtration and more even heat.</p>"},
